# Script Size and Structure

- No single Python script may exceed **700 lines**. If it does, refactor immediately.
- When a script grows beyond the limit, break it into a **`scripts/` folder** with a `main.py` coordinator that imports and orchestrates the sub-modules.

## When to Break Up

| Signal | Action |
|---|---|
| Script exceeds 700 lines | Mandatory split |
| Script has 3+ unrelated classes | Split by class responsibility |
| Script mixes menu logic with business logic | Separate into `menu.py` + domain modules |
| Script has utility functions used by others | Extract into `utils.py` |

## Folder Layout After Breakup

```
scripts/
  __init__.py
  main.py          # coordinator — the ONLY entry point
  menu.py          # questionary menus (if interactive)
  feature_a.py     # one concern per module
  feature_b.py
  utils.py         # shared helpers
```

### Rules for the breakup

- **`main.py` is the single entry point.** It imports and orchestrates sub-modules. No other script is called directly.
- **Each sub-module owns one concern** and is independently testable.
- **The parent Makefile calls `scripts/main.py`**, never individual sub-modules.
- **Menu logic lives in its own module** (`menu.py` or `menus/`), not mixed into domain classes.
- **Imports between sub-modules** go one direction: `main.py` imports everything else, domain modules import `utils.py`, never circular.

## How to Check

Before committing, verify no script exceeds the limit:

```bash
find scripts/ -name '*.py' -exec awk 'END{if(NR>700) print FILENAME, NR" lines"}' {} \;
```

## Example — Before and After

**Before** (one 900-line script):

```
my_tool.py  # 900 lines — menus, API calls, file I/O, all in one
```

**After** (broken into a scripts folder):

```
scripts/
  __init__.py
  main.py          # 80 lines — orchestrator, entry point
  menu.py          # 120 lines — questionary menus
  api_client.py    # 150 lines — API interaction class
  file_handler.py  # 100 lines — file read/write class
  utils.py         # 60 lines — shared constants and helpers
```
